ber 1965 connery admitted to  reports that he was already fed up to  here with the whole bond bit  though conory said at the time that he  shouldn't knock bond and said in a  november  1964 interview with the new york times  that he'd be everlastingly grateful to  the character his attitude changed over  the years  by the time he was shooting you only  lived twice released in 1967  connery had fallen out with producer  albert cubby broccoli who'd been  instrumental in getting him hired it  didn't help that he was stalked and  chased by paparazzi during filming or  that he felt his salary was too low you  only live twice was meant to be  connery's last bond appearance but he  returned for 1971's diamonds are forever  though not out of love for the character  in a 1971 interview with the guardian  connery made it clear that he resented  the studio executives for wasting his  time  i'd been messed about too much on other  bond pictures there's so much bull  that comes from bad decisions being made  at the top connery struck a deal to be  paid ten thousand dollars per week if  the film shoot ran over the scheduled 18  weeks he added  talking to some of these moguls about it  is like trying to describe to someone  who has never taken exercise  what it is like to feel fit when you do  exercise they don't understand  apparently connory only agreed to make  the movie having skipped 1969's on her  majesty's secret service  because he was given a large percentage  of profits and the opportunity to  produce two movies of his choice  and connery wasn't done with bond money  yet he returned for the appropriately  titled never say never again  reportedly for around five million  dollars amidst all that connery made it  clear how he felt about the character  that launched his career via the  guardian  i've always hated that damned james bond  i'd like to kill him  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite stars are coming soon